NAME
    head — Print the beginning of a file, because reading the whole
           thing is apparently a commitment.

SYNOPSIS
    head <file> [-n lines]

DESCRIPTION
    The 'head' command prints the first few lines of a text file.

    By default, it displays the first 10 lines. Because sometimes you
    don't need the whole 8,000-line log file—you just need to confirm
    that everything is, in fact, on fire.

    The number of lines can be customized with '-n'.

    Files are read as UTF-8 text, with invalid characters replaced
    rather than causing a dramatic terminal incident.

ARGUMENTS
    file
        The file whose beginning you wish to inspect.

        Required. 'head' has no interest in guessing which file you
        meant.

OPTIONS
    -n lines
        Number of lines to print.

        Defaults to 10.

        Set this to a reasonable number, or don't. The command will
        faithfully obey your questionable decisions.

EXAMPLES
    > head README.md
        Prints the first 10 lines of README.md.

        Enough to decide whether you actually want to read it.

    > head -n 5 README.md
        Prints only the first 5 lines.

        Five lines. Surely nothing important happens after that.

    > head -n 50 application.log
        Prints the first 50 lines of the log.

        Because 10 lines weren't nearly enough evidence.

    > head config.yaml
        Shows the top of your configuration file.

        The place where indentation-based suffering begins.

    > head -n 0 enormous.log
        Prints nothing.

        Technically flawless. Spiritually questionable.

ERRORS
    head error: <error>
        The file could not be opened or read.

        Possible causes include:
        - The file doesn't exist.
        - You don't have permission to read it.
        - The path is wrong.
        - The filesystem has once again chosen violence.

USAGE ERRORS
    If the arguments cannot be parsed, 'head' quietly exits.

    No lecture. No drama. Just silence.

OUTPUT
    Up to the requested number of lines are printed exactly as they
    appear in the file.

    Line endings are preserved during output.

    Invalid UTF-8 sequences are replaced with a replacement character,
    allowing the command to continue despite the file's best attempts
    at sabotage.

FUN FACTS
    - The default is 10 lines, because tradition apparently decided
      that 10 is the universal number of "just enough."
    - Unlike 'cat', 'head' knows when to stop.
    - File completion is enabled, because remembering filenames is a
      job better delegated to the computer.
    - Passing a negative number is technically allowed by the argument
      parser, but may result in the command printing nothing. Consider
      this a philosophical feature.
    - 'head' is especially useful for logs, CSVs, configuration files,
      and determining whether a file starts with something regrettable.

